<DocsPage
  title="Harnesses"
  description="Gimbal drives coding-agent harnesses. It does not replace them, and it is not a feature of any one of them."
  eyebrow="Feature"
  {headings}
>

A harness is the thing that actually runs an agent: its tools, its sandbox, its conversation store, its sign-in. Gimbal sits above it and decides who is asked what, when, and with which context.

<h2 id="shipped">Adapters in the box</h2>

| Package | Harness | How |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `gimbal/codex` | Codex | Attaches to the machine's shared `codex app-server` daemon. |
| `gimbal/claude` | Claude Code | Through the Claude Agent SDK. |
| `gimbal/agy` | Google Antigravity | Through the `agy` print-mode CLI. |
| `gimbal/pi` | Pi with Diffusion Router | Through Pi's RPC mode, with Gimbal-owned config and session storage. |

Your existing sign-in, configuration, and tools for each harness are what the agents run with.

For Pi, install Pi and put `pi` on `PATH`, then set `DIFFUSION_API_KEY`.
Select `pi/diffusion/<model-id>` with `gimbal run-prompt --model` or a
workflow role flag, for example `pi/diffusion/glm-5.3-flash`. Gimbal creates a
separate temporary config and session directory for each session; reasoning
effort is not supported for Pi models.

To find model IDs available to your Router key that support chat and tools:

```sh
curl -fsS https://router.diffusion.io/v1/models \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer ${DIFFUSION_API_KEY}" |
  jq -r '.data[] | select(.capabilities.openai_chat == true and .capabilities.tools == true) | .id'
```

Prefix a chosen open-weight ID with `pi/diffusion/` when binding a role.

<h2 id="binding">Binding roles to harnesses</h2>

A `ModelBinding` is a harness adapter, a model, and an effort. A run binds every role its workflow names, so one run can put planning on one vendor and review on another:

```sh
gimbal run pyramid-summary ... \
  --document-authoring gpt-5.6-sol:high \
  --editorial-review claude-opus-5:high
```

The workflow's source does not change. It named roles.

<h2 id="yours">Writing an adapter</h2>

<AdapterCode />

The interface is untyped on purpose: a raw JSON Schema goes in, raw JSON comes out, and `Generate` validates and decodes. Cancelling the context passed to `RunTurn` is the interrupt. `Steer` reports whether the message landed in a live turn. Whatever the adapter allocates for a session is released by `Close`, which the runtime calls when the owning scope ends.

</DocsPage>
